NAME
    dep - deposit bytes into memory

SYNOPSIS
    dep addr byte [byte ...]

DESCRIPTION
    Stores the hexadecimal byte values into memory from the hexadecimal
    address addr up. Quiet on success; "dep: bad address" when addr is
    not hex. The list ends at the end of the line or at the first word
    that is not hex; a value of more than two digits stores its low
    byte. The counterpart of dump. dep is /BIN/DEP.

LIMITS
    About 40 bytes per command (the 127-character argument tail).

EXAMPLES
    dep 6000 48 49 0A      "HI" and a line feed at $6000
    dep 6000 A9 01 60      three bytes of code

NOTES
    dep writes anywhere in the 64K space, with no guard rails: over the
    OS ($1000..$4FFF), the monitor's variables ($0F00 page) and over
    dep itself, which runs from $5000. Deposit well above it, from
    $6000 up, unless you mean it.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/dep.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    dump, examine, save, load, mon
